{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an ACL ARR peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**. You must be thorough, critical, and fair.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"When Does Cross-Lingual Calibration Transfer Work? Geometric Analysis and Practical Solutions\"\n\n**Core idea**: First large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer. Trains English logistic regression probes on hidden-state activations, then evaluates zero-shot transfer to 20 languages across 3 resource tiers (HIGH/MID/LOW) on 2 MoE architectures (Qwen3-235B-A22B, Llama 4 Scout 17B-16E). Evaluates 5 transfer methods: zero-shot, mean-shift, CaCLR (MMD consistency loss), linear projection (Procrustes), few-shot fine-tuning.\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. Depth-transferability Pareto frontier is architecture-dependent: Qwen3 shows strong tradeoff (16/20 langs negative depth-transfer ρ, median −0.42), Llama 4 weaker (13/20, median −0.28). Linked to CKA cliff steepness.\n2. CaCLR (systematic negative result): MMD consistency loss across full β sweep yields ΔAUROC=+0.002 (p=0.42). Geometric explanation via CKA subspace analysis.\n3. Linear projection with ~70 parallel pairs reaches 95% of max improvement. Procrustes effect reversal at ~0.55.\n4. ~30% LOW-resource and ~28% MID-resource probing results are capacity artifacts.\n5. Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer correlation.\n\n**Related Work sections covered**:\n- LLM Calibration: Joshi et al. [2024], Guo et al. [2017], LACE Zhou et al. [2024]\n- Representation-Based Calibration: CORAL Miao et al. [2026a], Miao & Ungar [2026], Ji et al. [2024]\n- Cross-Lingual Probing: Xiao et al. [2025] (knowledge-boundary probes, 8 languages), Civelli et al. (difficulty transfer)\n- Cross-Lingual Alignment: MUSE Conneau et al. [2018], VecMap Artetxe et al. [2018]\n- Domain Adaptation Theory: Ben-David et al. [2010], Zhao et al. [2019]\n- Probing Methodology: Hewitt & Liang [2019], Voita & Titov [2020]\n- Representation Similarity: CKA Kornblith et al. [2019], SVCCA Raghu et al. [2017]\n- Conformal Prediction: Angelopoulos and Bates [2023]\n\n**Paper structure** (14 pages):\n1. Introduction (1 page)\n2. Related Work (1 page, 8 subsections)\n3. Method (1.5 pages: §3.1 Cross-Lingual Correctness Probing, §3.2 Transfer Methods, §3.3 Difficulty-Capability Decomposition, §3.4 CKA Geometric Analysis)\n4. Experiments (6 pages: §4.1 Setup, §4.2 Pareto Frontier, §4.3 CKA Analysis, §4.4 CaCLR Negative Result, §4.5 Transfer Comparison, §4.6 Linear Projection Efficiency, §4.7 Difficulty Decomposition, §4.8 Control Tasks, §4.9 Supporting Analyses [orthogonality, LR vs MLP, cross-benchmark, quantization], §4.10 Extended Evaluation [Gemma4, 52 langs])\n5. Discussion (1 page)\n6. Conclusion (0.5 pages)\nReferences: 15 entries\n\n**Key methodological contributions claimed**:\n- First large-scale cross-lingual correctness probe transfer study (vs Xiao et al.'s 8-language knowledge-boundary probes)\n- CaCLR negative result with geometric explanation grounded in Zhao et al.'s theory\n- First application of Hewitt & Liang control tasks to cross-lingual probing\n- Procrustes effect reversal threshold as practitioner guidance\n- Simpson's Paradox discovery in CKA-transfer correlation\n\n**Transfer methods**:\n- Zero-shot: apply EN probe directly\n- Mean-shift: subtract mean activation difference (ΔAUROC ≡ 0 by construction for LR)\n- CaCLR: L = L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_MMD (novel formulation)\n- Linear Projection: Procrustes alignment W* = argmin ||H^en - H^tgt W||²_F\n- Few-shot: retrain on k target-language labeled examples\n\n**Models and scale**: 2 MoE architectures (Qwen3-235B-A22B with INT4, Llama 4 Scout with W4A16), 20 languages in 3 tiers, 3 benchmarks. Camera-ready adds Gemma 4 31B Dense and 52 languages.\n\n## ACL ARR Scoring Criteria\n\n**Soundness** (1-5):\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims, minor points may need extra support\n- 2 = Poor: main claims not sufficiently supported\n- 1 = Major Issues\n\n**Excitement** (1-5):\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: would recommend to others\n- 4 = Exciting: would mention to others\n- 3 = Interesting: might mention some points\n- 2 = Potentially Interesting\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall** (1-5):\n- 5 = Consider for Award\n- 4 = Conference (*ACL main)\n- 3 = Findings\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a thorough review focusing on **novelty, framing, and related work**. Structure your review as:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (numbered, focus on novelty, positioning relative to prior work, theoretical grounding, contribution significance)\n3. **Weaknesses** (numbered, focus on novelty limitations, missing related work, overclaimed contributions, framing issues, gaps in positioning)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5, with 0.5 increments), Excitement (1-5), Overall (1-5)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5): 1=low, 5=expert\n\nConsider these angles:\n- Is the correctness vs. knowledge-boundary distinction sufficiently motivated? Does it matter in practice?\n- How novel is the Procrustes alignment approach given MUSE/VecMap literature?\n- Is the CaCLR negative result genuinely informative or somewhat predictable?\n- Are the architectural conclusions meaningful with n=2 (core) or n=3 (extended)?\n- Does the paper adequately position against concurrent work in multilingual probing?\n- Is the Simpson's Paradox finding a genuine insight or a known confounding issue?\n- How significant are the practical contributions (the threshold, the ~70 pairs guidance)?\n\nBe specific and cite section numbers. Calibrate to a strong *ACL venue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7yTddsvZnhyAKbGetqXdY","session_id":"91a2196c-7a23-4294-80ba-646c05703e7b","uuid":"