{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011ubXZr5cLWYP2oezHBhDKL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B7kGBMPK69ASVcAZkUNYWs","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T044911/best/paper.tex","offset":0,"limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":26332,"cache_read_input_tokens":47085,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":26332,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7yTddsvZnhyAKbGetqXdY","session_id":"91a2196c-7a23-4294-80ba-646c05703e7b","uuid":"ff394c8d-0a64-4e20-b1ac-05103f4fed3c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B7kGBMPK69ASVcAZkUNYWs","type":"tool_result","content":"0\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n1\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n2\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n3\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n4\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{array}\n10\t\\usepackage{caption}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\n13\t\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\rmark}{\\textcolor{red}{$\\rho$}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\pp}{\\,\\text{pp}}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Geometric Analysis Reveals When and Why\\\\Cross-Lingual Calibration Transfer Succeeds}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t%==============================================================================\n30\tProbe-based calibration methods can detect whether a language model's internal representations encode answer correctness, but all existing approaches are English-only. We present the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer, spanning three architectures (Qwen3-235B, Llama~4 Scout, Gemma~4 31B Dense), 50 languages, and five transfer conditions. Our analysis reveals four interconnected findings forming a causal chain. \\textbf{(1)}~A depth-transferability Pareto frontier whose strength is architecture-dependent: Qwen3 exhibits a strong, consistent tradeoff (per-language Spearman $\\rho$ significant in 15/20 languages) while Llama~4 shows a weak, language-specific pattern (6/20 significant; three languages exhibit reversed trends). \\textbf{(2)}~Decomposing correctness into difficulty-shared (65\\% variance, 12\\% of transfer degradation) and capability-specific (35\\% variance, 88\\% of degradation) components reveals that capability-specific representations drive deep-layer transfer failure. \\textbf{(3)}~CaCLR, a consistency-regularized calibration method requiring no target-language labels, provides a mean improvement of +3.5\\pp{} AUROC over zero-shot transfer, with gains concentrated at deep layers (+4.8\\pp). \\textbf{(4)}~A Simpson's Paradox in the CKA--transfer relationship---raw correlation is negative ($\\rho = -0.36$ to $-0.41$; depth confound) but partial correlation controlling for depth is significantly positive ($\\rho = +0.41$ to $+0.48$; $p < 0.01$ on both architectures)---reveals that within-layer cross-lingual CKA genuinely predicts transfer quality while the correctness direction occupies a near-universal subspace (within-layer angle $12.4^{\\circ}$). We additionally find that 28\\% of low-resource probing results are capacity artifacts, and that Procrustes alignment reverses direction at a threshold of $\\sim$0.52 zero-shot AUROC. Code and probes will be released.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%==============================================================================\n34\t\\section{Introduction}\n35\t\\label{sec:intro}\n36\t%==============================================================================\n37\t\n38\tMultilingual large language models suffer from a persistent calibration gap: non-English languages exhibit expected calibration error (ECE) up to five times worse than English \\cite{joshi2024llm}. Output-based recalibration methods such as temperature scaling require per-language calibration data, while ensemble approaches like MKA incur multiplicative inference costs \\cite{zhou2024lace}. Recent work on probe-based calibration offers an alternative: linear probes trained on internal representations can detect whether a model ``knows'' the answer is correct, and this correctness direction is nearly orthogonal to the model's verbalized confidence \\cite{miao2026coral,miao2026steering}. However, the entire probe-based calibration literature is English-only.\n39\t\n40\tCan English-trained correctness probes transfer to other languages without target-language labels? If so, under what conditions, and why does transfer succeed or fail? These questions are fundamental to the viability of representation-level calibration beyond English. Xiao et al.\\ \\cite{xiao2025crosslingual} transferred \\emph{knowledge boundary} probes---binary answerability classifiers at the input level---and found encouraging results in 8 languages. But correctness probing targets a fundamentally different signal: output-dependent accuracy on specific items, not input-level answerability. The cross-lingual properties of correctness representations remain unexplored.\n41\t\n42\tWe present the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer. Probing three architectures (Qwen3-235B-A22B, Llama~4 Scout, Gemma~4 31B Dense) across 20--50 typologically diverse languages on GlobalMMLU, we discover a \\emph{depth-transferability Pareto frontier}: deeper layers yield better English probes but progressively degrade in zero-shot cross-lingual transfer. The frontier's strength is architecture-dependent---strong and consistent on Qwen3, weak and language-specific on Llama~4. A geometric analysis based on centered kernel alignment (CKA) reveals a Simpson's Paradox at the heart of this phenomenon: pooling across layers, CKA and transfer quality correlate \\emph{negatively} (depth confound), but controlling for layer depth, higher within-layer CKA significantly predicts better transfer ($p < 0.01$ on both architectures). The correctness direction itself occupies a near-universal low-dimensional subspace (cross-language angle $12.4^{\\circ}$ within each layer) that persists even as global representations specialize by language.\n43\t\n44\tOur contributions are:\n45\t\\begin{enumerate}\n46\t\\item \\textbf{Pareto discovery.} We identify an architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff across three architectures and 20 languages, with Pareto knee at 55--58\\% depth.\n47\t\\item \\textbf{Decomposition analysis.} We decompose correctness into difficulty-shared and capability-specific components, showing that capability drives 88\\% of transfer degradation despite accounting for only 35\\% of variance.\n48\t\\item \\textbf{CaCLR method.} A consistency-regularized calibration approach providing +3.5\\pp{} mean AUROC gain without target-language labels, with gains concentrated at deep layers.\n49\t\\item \\textbf{CKA geometric explanation.} The Simpson's Paradox, subspace persistence ($12.4^{\\circ}$ within-layer, $38.7^{\\circ}$ cross-layer), and CKA cliff threshold ($\\sim$0.35) together explain when and why transfer succeeds."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7yTddsvZnhyAKbGetqXdY","session_id":"91a2196c-7a23-4294-80ba-646c05703e7b","uuid":"79d84afe-5efd-4572-a7c9-0bcf4bd53a6b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:01:28.647Z"}
